   "id": "h2-51",
   "name": "Scurvy and anaemia"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Scurvy is caused by a lack of vitamin C (ascorbic acid).\nSigns: bleeding, swollen and spongy gums, loose teeth, wounds that heal slowly, bruises, tiredness, painful joints.\nSources of vitamin C: oranges, lemons, grapefruit, guavas, pawpaw, pineapple, mangoes, tomatoes, green leafy vegetables. Vitamin C is destroyed by long cooking, so eat fruit raw.\nAnaemia is a lack of red blood cells or haemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen.\nCauses: lack of iron (and folic acid, vitamin B12) in the diet; loss of blood (worms, heavy periods, wounds); malaria.\nSigns: pale eyelids, palms and tongue; tiredness; dizziness; breathlessness; fast heartbeat.\nSources of iron: liver, red meat, beans, dark green leaves (njama-njama, okok), eggs, fish. Vitamin C helps the body absorb iron.\nPrevention: a balanced diet, deworming, sleeping under a mosquito net.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "A pupil is always tired, has pale eyelids and gets breathless easily. What may she have, and which foods would help?",
  "steps": [
   "Pale eyelids, tiredness and breathlessness are signs of anaemia.",
   "She needs iron: liver, beans, dark green leaves, with fruit for vitamin C."
  ],
  "answer": "Anaemia: iron-rich foods (liver, beans, green leaves) with fruit.",
  "checks": []
 },
